    {
      "id": "CVE-2026-104852",
      "summary": "Closed by https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/pull/8423\nMitigated in Hive Gateway by https://github.com/graphql-hive/gateway/pull/2600\n\nA client can alias fields to `constructor`, `__proto__` or `prototype` so that the response keys from two subgraphs collide on those names during result merging. Because `mergeDeep` recursed through inherited properties, the merge walked `{}.constructor` to `Object`, then `Object.__proto__` to `Function.prototype`, and wrote a subgraph-supplied value over `Function.prototype.call`, breaking every subsequent request in the process until restart. This is remotely triggerable by an unauthenticated client with a single query against any supergraph that merges an object from two subgraphs, which is the ordinary `@shareable` or entity case, so it is a denial of service rather than a theoretical hardening issue.\n\n```graphql\n{\n  shared {\n    fieldA\n    constructor: fieldB {\n      __proto__: child {\n        call: value\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
      "cvssScore": 8.2,
      "severity": "High",
      "published": "2026-10-05",
      "modified": "2026-10-05",
      "cwe": {
        "cwe_id": "CWE-1321",
        "name": "Improperly Controlled Modification of Object Prototype Attributes ('Prototype Pollution')"
      },
      "references": [
        "https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/security/advisories/GHSA-7mx3-vvmw-hjmv",
        "https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/pull/8423",
        "https://github.com/graphql-hive/gateway/pull/2600",
        "https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/commit/0b9529f1988fd36186a7c106a6efe0356f1b7f2e",
        "https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/releases/tag/@graphql-tools/utils@12.0.1"
      ],
      "vulnerableProducts": [
        "@graphql-tools/utils npm"
      ],
      "isExploited": false
    },
